"A different objection has become prominent in recent decades. The philosopher of universal human dignity also lectured for years on anthropology and physical geography, and in those lectures and some essays he ranked the peoples of the world in a hierarchy of races with Europeans at the top. In his later political writings he condemned colonial conquest and slavery. Scholars disagree about whether he changed his mind or held both views at once. Either way, his case shows how a thinker can state a principle that condemns his own prejudice and fail for most of his life to apply it.": "92b7ccd4f8a0",
"Before Kant, philosophers assumed that knowledge is the minds conforming to objects, and they could not explain how we know anything about objects in advance, as we seem to in mathematics and in the principle that every event has a cause. Kant proposed to try the opposite assumption. Copernicus, unable to explain the motions of the heavens while supposing that they revolve around the spectator, tried looking for the observed movements not in the heavenly bodies, but in the spectator. Suppose, likewise, that objects conform to our way of knowing. Space, time, and causation are then the forms that the mind brings to experience. We can know in advance that everything we meet will be in space and time and subject to causal law, because nothing could be an experience for us otherwise. The price is that we know things only as they appear to beings like us. What they are in themselves we cannot know. Both halves are needed: Thoughts without content are void; intuitions without conceptions, blind.": "21cd894046ae",
"By then his life had taken the fixed form for which he is remembered. He rose at five, drank tea, smoked one pipe, lectured from seven, wrote until one, and dined at length with guests chosen from outside the university, since he disliked talking philosophy at table. At half past three he walked, alone and breathing through his nose, and it is said that his neighbors set their clocks by him. The one recorded lapse came when he was reading Rousseaus Emile and could not put it down. Rousseaus portrait hung in his study, the only picture in the house. It was Rousseau, he wrote, who had cured him of despising the ignorant and taught him to honor human beings.": "28f21e9225bb",
"He entered the local university at sixteen and discovered Newton. When his father died he had to leave without a post, and for nine years he worked as a tutor in country houses. He returned in 1755 as an unsalaried lecturer, paid by the students who chose to attend, and he lectured for fifteen years on almost everything: logic, physics, mathematics, geography, anthropology, fortification. In those years he was known as an elegant and witty man, a good hand at billiards and cards and a sought-after dinner guest. He proposed, in a work on astronomy, that the solar system had condensed from a rotating cloud of gas, a hypothesis that is still accepted in outline.": "d6a83d50e3ac",
"He was fifty-seven, and the works that made him the central figure of modern philosophy were all still to come. They followed within a decade: the Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals in 1785, a second edition of the first Critique in 1787, the Critique of Practical Reason in 1788, and the Critique of Judgment in 1790.": "1cb5728e1830",
"He welcomed the American and French revolutions and went on defending the principles of the second after the Terror had turned most of his countrymen against it. In 1794 his book on religion brought a rebuke from the Prussian king and an order to write no more on the subject, which he obeyed until the king died. His mind began to fail in his last years. He died on February 12, 1804. His last words, on being given a little wine and water, were It is good. The city turned out for the funeral.": "fe7dcd3ccb0c",
"His books raise a different problem of access. He wrote in a technical vocabulary that he largely invented, and he revised his central work so heavily for its second edition that scholars cite the two by letter, A and B, and still argue about which represents his real view. He knew that he was hard to read and said that the fault lay in the subject.": "23926a708c5a",
"In 1770, at forty-six, he was at last given the chair of logic and metaphysics. Then he published almost nothing for eleven years. He had read David Hume, and Humes argument that we have no rational ground for believing in causation had, he wrote, first interrupted my dogmatic slumber. If Hume was right, science had no foundation. If Hume was wrong, someone had to show how the mind can know in advance of experience that every event has a cause. Kants friends expected a book each year, and each year he wrote that it was nearly ready. The Critique of Pure Reason appeared in 1781, eight hundred pages put on paper, by his own account, in four or five months after twelve years of thought. The first reviewers found it unreadable.": "dbc92388899a",
"Kant was born in 1724 in Königsberg, a Baltic port at the eastern edge of the German-speaking world, and he died there seventy-nine years later without ever having traveled more than a few days journey from it. His father made harnesses. The family belonged to the Pietists, a Lutheran movement that valued inward devotion, plain living, and strict self-examination, and Kant said in old age that no one could have had a better moral education than his parents gave him. The school the Pietists ran was another matter. He remembered its regime of prayer and drill with something like horror, and it left him with a lifelong dislike of religious enthusiasm.": "293294306a2c",
"Kants distinction between things as they appear and things in themselves has often been set beside the Buddhist two truths, and the comparison needs care. For Kant there is a reality behind appearances that we cannot reach. For Nāgārjuna there is nothing hidden behind the conventional world, and the ultimate truth is the emptiness of that very world. In ethics the two share a universal scope and part over the role of feeling. Kant distrusts inclination and locates moral worth in duty done against it. The Buddhist path is a training of inclination, so that kindness is finally what one wants to do. And Kant holds that morality needs a free and responsible self as its postulate, which is a place where the teaching of not-self would press him hard (Dharma and the West, D10 and D21).": "c368ea270b63",
"The Kant of legend, a thinking machine who never altered his walk, comes mostly from three memoirs published by acquaintances in the year of his death, which describe the routines of a man over sixty, and from a brilliant page of the poet Heinrich Heine, written thirty years later, which says that he had neither life nor history. The record of his first forty-five years is thinner and shows someone else: a popular lecturer who was told by a friend that he spent too much time at dinners and card tables to write the books he was capable of. The regularity was adopted in middle age, on the advice of an English merchant friend, by a man with a weak chest who had decided that he had work to finish.": "2154dd59177b",
"The charge made in his own lifetime was that the theory is too rigid to live by. When Benjamin Constant asked whether one must tell the truth to a murderer seeking ones friend, Kant answered that one must, and the essay in which he said so has embarrassed his admirers for two hundred years. Hegel pressed a deeper version. The test of universal law, he argued, is empty. It can show that a maxim is consistent, and almost any maxim can be made consistent if it is described carefully. The real content of morality comes from the customs and institutions of an actual community, and Kant has mistaken the form of morality for its source. A third criticism comes from Aristotles side. A person who visits a sick friend because duty requires it has, most of us feel, missed something that the friend who comes out of affection has not.": "95eb563a9942",
"The closest meeting is over the questions that cannot be answered. The Buddha was asked whether the world is eternal or not, finite or infinite, and whether the soul is the same as the body or different, and he set the questions aside as undetermined. Kants first Critique contains a section called the Antinomies, in which he takes the same questions, whether the world has a beginning in time and a limit in space, and proves both the yes and the no with equal rigor. His conclusion is that reason contradicts itself when it tries to speak of the world as a whole, because it has gone beyond any possible experience. The two give different reasons for the silence. The Buddha says that the questions do not lead to the end of suffering. Kant says that the human mind is not equipped to settle them. The Indian philosopher T. R. V. Murti argued in 1955 that the Madhyamaka schools method, which shows that every view about ultimate reality leads to contradiction, is the nearest thing in world philosophy to Kants critique.": "795756f3ccac",
"The same move governs his ethics. The moral law is not handed to us by God, nature, or society. A rational being gives it to itself, and Kants word for this is autonomy, self-legislation. To act morally is to act on a principle that one can will as a law for all rational beings, and to be capable of this is to have a worth that nothing can be traded for. Things have a price. Persons have dignity. Near the end of the second Critique he set the two halves of his philosophy side by side, in the sentence that was later cut on his tomb: Two things fill the mind with ever new and increasing admiration and awe, the oftener and the more steadily we reflect on them: the starry heavens above and the moral law within.": "25475c278c5e",
